# BERT Architecture and Working (In-depth but Easy)

## 1) Big picture: what are Transformer models?

Transformer models process text using **self-attention**.

### Core idea

Instead of reading words one-by-one like an RNN, Transformer reads **all tokens together** and decides:

* which words are important to each other
* how strongly they are related

Example sentence:

> "The bank approved the loan because it was safe."

Self-attention helps the model learn that:

* “it” refers to “loan” (not bank)

---

## 2) Tokens and embeddings (common to BERT & GPT)

Before going inside the model, both do the same preprocessing:

### Step A: Tokenization

Text is split into tokens (subwords).
Example:

* "unbelievable" → ["un", "##bel", "##ievable"]

### Step B: Embedding vectors

Each token becomes a vector:

* **Token embedding** (meaning of the token)
* **Position embedding** (where it occurs in the sentence)

### Step C: Attention mask

Mask decides what tokens can attend to:

* BERT: can attend to **both left and right** tokens (bidirectional)
* GPT: can attend only to **left tokens** (causal)

---

# 3) BERT Architecture (Encoder-only Transformer)

## What is BERT?

**BERT = Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers**

BERT is a **Transformer Encoder stack**.

### Key property

✅ **Bidirectional attention**: each token attends to *both* past and future tokens.

This makes BERT very good at:

* understanding text
* classification
* NER
* semantic similarity
* retrieval tasks

---

## 3.1 BERT Input format (important)

BERT uses special tokens:

* **[CLS]**: classification token (represents entire sequence)
* **[SEP]**: separator between sentences
* **[PAD]**: padding

Example: sentence pair

```
[CLS] sentence A [SEP] sentence B [SEP]
```

### Segment embeddings

BERT adds token-type embeddings:

* Sentence A tokens = segment 0
* Sentence B tokens = segment 1

---

## 3.2 Inside a BERT encoder layer

Each encoder block has:

### (1) Multi-Head Self Attention (MHSA)

Self-attention learns relationships:

* Query (Q)
* Key (K)
* Value (V)

Computation idea (simplified):

* similarity(Q,K) → attention weights
* weighted sum of V → output representation

Multi-head means:

* model learns different relationship types in parallel

### (2) Add & Norm (Residual connection)

* output = LayerNorm(input + attention_output)

### (3) Feed Forward Network (FFN)

A small MLP applied to each token independently:

* Linear → activation (GELU) → Linear

### (4) Add & Norm again

---

## 3.3 How BERT learns (Pretraining objectives)

BERT was trained on unsupervised text using 2 objectives:

### A) Masked Language Modeling (MLM)

Random tokens are replaced by:

* [MASK] 80%
* random token 10%
* unchanged 10%

Model predicts missing words using both context sides.

Example:

> "I live in [MASK]." → “Bangalore”

### B) Next Sentence Prediction (NSP)

Given two sentences:

* Are they consecutive in original text?

Note: Many later BERT variants removed NSP.

---

## 3.4 Why BERT is powerful

Because it is **bidirectional**, it builds strong contextual embeddings.

Example:

* "He sat on the *bank*" → river bank meaning
* "He went to the *bank*" → finance bank meaning

BERT produces different embeddings based on context.

---

# 4) GPT Architecture (Decoder-only Transformer)

## What is GPT?

**GPT = Generative Pre-trained Transformer**

GPT is a **Transformer Decoder stack**.

### Key property

✅ **Causal attention** (left-to-right)

* token *t* can only attend to tokens ≤ t

This makes GPT very good at:

* text generation
* dialogue
* summarization
* code generation
* reasoning

---

## 4.1 GPT decoder layer structure

It looks similar to BERT layer, but has a crucial difference:

### (1) Masked Multi-Head Self Attention

Attention uses a **triangular mask** so future tokens are blocked.

### (2) Add & Norm

### (3) Feed Forward Network

### (4) Add & Norm

---

## 4.2 GPT training objective

GPT trains using:

### Autoregressive Language Modeling (CLM)

Predict next token:

Example:
Input:

> "I live in"

Model predicts:

> " Bangalore"

Loss is calculated over every position:

* predict token2 from token1
* predict token3 from token1 token2
* ...

---

## 4.3 Why GPT is good at generation

Because it is trained exactly like generation:

* it always predicts next token
* during inference, it keeps appending predicted tokens

---

# 5) BERT vs GPT — main differences (most important section)

## 5.1 Encoder-only vs Decoder-only

* **BERT**: Encoder stack only
* **GPT**: Decoder stack only

## 5.2 Attention type

* **BERT**: bidirectional self-attention
* **GPT**: causal self-attention

## 5.3 Objective

* **BERT**: MLM (fill mask)
* **GPT**: Next-token prediction

## 5.4 Output behavior

* **BERT**: produces embeddings (good for understanding)
* **GPT**: generates new text (good for creation)

---

# 6) Easy intuition (Tamil + English style 🤝)

### BERT = "Full sentence reader"

BERT reads full sentence like:

* "first understand entire meaning"
* then answer classification/label tasks

### GPT = "Story writer"

GPT works like:

* "keep writing next word"
* doesn’t peek future words

---

# 7) Typical Use-cases

## BERT use cases

* Sentiment classification
* Intent classification
* Named Entity Recognition
* Text similarity (Siamese BERT)
* Search ranking / retrieval

## GPT use cases

* Chatbots
* Summarization
* Translation
* Code generation
* Content creation

---

# 8) Simple architecture diagram (text version)

## BERT (Encoder-only)

```
Tokens → Embeddings → [Encoder]xN → Contextual embeddings
                           │
                           ├─ [CLS] embedding → classification head
                           └─ token embeddings → tagging heads
```

## GPT (Decoder-only)

```
Tokens → Embeddings → [Masked Decoder]xN → Next-token probabilities
                                              │
                                              └→ generate token by token
```

---

# 9) When to choose which?

### Choose BERT when you want:

* accurate classification
* strong semantic representations
* understanding tasks

### Choose GPT when you want:

* generation
* reasoning and step-by-step answers
* interactive dialogue

---

# 10) Summary (1-minute)

* **BERT** = Encoder-only, bidirectional, MLM → best for understanding
* **GPT** = Decoder-only, causal, next-token LM → best for generation

